      "id": "50110159",
      "title": "Show HN: Turbo Lisp – A Common Lisp IDE for macOS and the Terminal",
      "url": "https://gitlab.com/patchwork1-ai/turbolisp",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50110159",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "points": 2,
      "comments": 0,
      "author": "stray",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-15T21:00:36Z",
      "storyText": "When Apple left Clozure Common Lisp behind by switching to Apple Silicon, I started kicking around the idea of building my own Lisp&#x2F;Objective-C bridge by combining Apple Documentation, the clang AST, and introspection of the Objective-C runtime.<p>There was a CCL porting effort, but I really wanted to use SBCL anyway.<p>Truth be told — what I really wanted was a 21st-century CLIM variant on top of modern AppKit 26. So I let that idea rattle around in my skull for a while — and eventually it bounced up against the memory of Ck (Curses-based Tk).<p>Anybody remember that?<p>I liked Tcl&#x2F;Tk and [incr Tcl] and it had always stuck in my head how great an idea it was to have something along the lines of CLIM for regular terminals as well as GUI.<p>So anyway, I stumbled upon the open-source CLIM2 source still maintained by Franz, and the wheels commenced to turn.<p>Oh yeah, and my very first programming environment had been Turbo Pascal. That integrated debugger was really something.<p>And y&#x27;know, it all started to come together as an idea about modernizing CLIM and modifying Portable Hemlock to run on top of it — and integrating a Debugger with conditional breakpoints and Step In&#x2F;Step Out&#x2F;Step Over, CLIM presentations, Inspector...<p>And in years past, I&#x27;d have just dreamed about it.<p>But the past three years have been crazy. GPT4 was the first time I seriously tried to build things with an LLM. And I&#x27;ve been pushing the LLMs as far as they would go to build my intuition mainly.<p>But up until this year they&#x27;ve been mostly crap.<p>I used &quot;Build Common Lisp&quot; as my north star because the Dietz ANSI tests seemed like a nearly perfect product specification.<p>Long story short: I threw away 7 attempts before I won ANSI — <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;gitlab.com&#x2F;patchwork1-ai&#x2F;whateverlisp\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;gitlab.com&#x2F;patchwork1-ai&#x2F;whateverlisp</a> — and while I was waiting for Claude and Codex to grovel through ANSI I was trying my hand at my grand idea of Turbo Lisp.<p>Side note: my high score on ANSI is 21,916&#x2F;21,925 passing (99.96%); 21,925 executed; 9 failing.  And those 9?  Yeah I don&#x27;t think you could pass those without failing 30+ other ones.<p>So anyway — It’s called *Turbo Lisp* (based on SBCL). It brings together a Listener, debugger, object inspector, conditional breakpoints + stepper, and Zeta—a variant of Portable Hemlock that uses WhateverCLIM as its front-end.<p>On macOS, it uses native AppKit UI through *Chattanooga, a Lisp&#x2F;Objective-C bridge I built*. It also runs in the terminal through *Oak Ridge, my Lisp port of Ratatui*. Both use *WhateverCLIM*, which is the CLIM2 code from Franz plus my own modernization, including *<i>presentations*</i>: displayed Lisp objects you can click and inspect.<p>*v0.2.1 patch release* is available for Apple Silicon Macs, Intel Macs, and Linux x86-64. The Mac builds currently require macOS 26.<p>I’m especially interested in hearing:<p>* Does it install and start properly on your machine?\n* Can you find your way around the Listener, editor, and debugger?\n* Does the terminal interface work with your terminal emulator?\n* Where does it feel confusing, awkward, or broken?<p>A report that says “I tried this and got stuck here” is useful. Please include your OS, CPU architecture, and terminal emulator where relevant.<p>It’s still early, and I’m looking for honest feedback from people using it outside my own setup.",
